'''Bile Sago''' (born '''Bile Masar'''; 11 May 1963) is a [[Burawa|Burawi]] politician who has served as the senator for the [[Dafar Sare Rural Administration]] since 2009. He is the founder and leader of the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]] (SRP), a regionalist and {{W|right-wing populism|populist}} party centred on the [[Sago]] region of western Burawa. Sago was previously a member of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]], from which he was expelled in 2016 and which he left permanently in 2020.

Born Bile Masar, he is a distant relative of the [[House of Masar]], the historic merchant family of [[Mazar]]. He adopted the surname ''Sago'' upon joining the National Party in the late 1990s.

== Early life and education ==

Bile Masar was born on 11 May 1963 in [[Kankadadka]], the capital of [[Burawa]], to [[Guled Masar]] and [[Filsan Masar]] (née Dheere). Both parents were party officers of the [[Kadarist Party]]; following the party's collapse after the [[1962 Crisis]], they became [[Tenant Farmers Party of Burawa|Tenant Farmers Party]] representatives in the capital. The Masar family was originally from the [[Sago]] area, and Sago grew up between Kankadadka and the family's home region.

For his [[Burawi Work Programme]] assignment, Sago was posted to [[Sago]] as an assistant to the regional railroad administrator. The assignment has been characterised as a comfortable posting secured through his family's political connections.

Sago entered politics at the age of eighteen in 1981, joining the Tenant Farmers Party. In 1985, his first wife was killed in the [[Kedam train derailment]] (see [[#First marriage|Personal life]]). After the Tenant Farmers Party's merger with the [[Christian Socialist Party of Burawa]] to form the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] in 1986, Sago left active politics. He enrolled at the [[University of Sago]], where he joined the faculty as a lecturer in economics, a position he held for approximately a decade.

== National Party career ==

=== Return to politics ===

The [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]] (CSU) split from the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] on 5 April 1996, following the passage of the [[Domestic Partnership Act of 1996 (Burawa)|Domestic Partnership Act]]. In rural [[Sago]], where the population was heavily Catholic and socially conservative, the CSU attracted significant support at the National Party's expense. Sago re-entered politics in the late 1990s and rejoined the National Party, adopting the surname ''Sago''.

=== Commune and Senate elections ===

Sago won election as the commune representative for '''Dafar Sare-1''', a commune within the [[Dafar Sare Rural Administration]], in 2000, succeeding [[Ladan Waarabe]].

In 2009, Sago launched a joint campaign for the Dafar Sare Rural Administration Senate seat alongside [[Ubax Koofur]] (later [[Ubax Sago]]), who stood simultaneously for Sago's commune seat in Dafar Sare-1. The campaign, branded the '''"Love Tour"''' ({{lang|bur|''Safarka Jacaylka''}}), was built around a public promise that if both candidates won their respective races, the couple would marry. Both candidates won — Sago defeated the incumbent [[Guuleed Dheere]] for the Senate seat, and Koofur won the commune seat — and the couple married shortly after taking office.

The Love Tour received extensive national media coverage and drew criticism from other parties, but Sago won re-election in successive Senate cycles. His rallies were characterised by elaborate staging and showmanship, including on at least one occasion performances by cabaret dancers.

=== Harvest Festival incident ===

In 2011, Sago was filmed in a state of intoxication at the annual [[Sago Harvest Festival]], delivering an impromptu speech that included personal attacks on the administrator of the [[Dafar Sare Rural Administration]]. The footage circulated nationally. Sago issued a public apology, attributing the incident to "passion for the people of Sago."

=== Expulsion and readmission ===

In 2016, the National Party expelled Sago. The expulsion followed a series of controversies, including the cabaret incidents at his rallies and an infidelity scandal involving reports of an unacknowledged son in [[Balisca]] (see [[#Second marriage and infidelity scandal|Personal life]]). Sago retained his Senate seat as an independent, as Burawi law does not require a senator to vacate a seat upon losing party affiliation. The [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|CSU]] approached Sago during this period, but he declined to join.

In 2018, the National Party readmitted Sago. The reconciliation proved short-lived.

== HSR Scandal ==

{{main|Burawi High Speed Rail Scandal}}

The [[Burawi High Speed Rail Scandal]] of 2019 implicated several northern rural representatives in a conspiracy to secure billions of [[hanti]] in funding for a parallel {{W|high-speed rail}} line between [[Sago]] and [[Lanwaley]], justified by falsified population growth projections. Two commune representatives within Sago's senate district were among those arrested.

Sago was not formally charged. He stated publicly that he had been approached by the conspirators but had declined to participate. Whether Sago provided information to investigators that contributed to the implication of his commune representatives has not been established.

The government's response to the scandal included collective sanctions against the [[Dhulsarren]] region, which encompasses the northern portion of Sago — funding restrictions, reduced growth allowances, and heightened scrutiny of regional representatives. Calls for Sago to be investigated continued through 2019 and 2020, contributing to his departure from the National Party.

== Formation of the Sago Revolutionary Party ==

=== Departure from the National Party ===

Sago left the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] in 2020. Three factors contributed to the departure: continuing pressure over his potential involvement in the [[Burawi High Speed Rail Scandal]]; the {{W|COVID-19 pandemic}}, during which Sago adopted {{W|vaccine hesitancy|anti-vaccination}} and {{W|conspiracy theory|conspiracy}} positions opposed by both the National Party and the [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|CSU]]; and the collective sanctions imposed on the [[Dhulsarren]], which provided a basis for a regional grievance platform.

=== Founding of the SRP ===

Sago founded the '''[[Sago Revolutionary Party]]''' (SRP) in 2020. The party's initial platform combined {{W|vaccine hesitancy|vaccine scepticism}} and opposition to pandemic restrictions with [[Sago]] regional nationalism. The [[Sago]] region had been part of [[Burawa]] for several centuries and had voted overwhelmingly — over 90 per cent — to remain in the republic during the [[Act of Self-Determination]] of 1934; the SRP's regionalism represented a new political identity rather than a continuation of an existing movement.

The party's ideology is broadly similar to that of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] — conservative socialism, agrarianism, protectionism — but includes more {{W|reactionary}} positions on social issues.

=== Electoral performance ===

Sago won re-election to the Dafar Sare Senate seat in the 2021 cycle under the SRP banner. The party also contested [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]] seats, winning 6 in the 2025 election. As of 2025, the party's representation is confined to the [[Dafar Sare Rural Administration]] and the [[Dafar Hoose Rural Administration]].

== Political style ==

=== Appearance ===

Sago is heavyset and broad-shouldered. He is recognised by a thick mustache that has become his most frequently {{W|political cartoon|caricatured}} feature. He dresses in traditional rural [[Sago]] clothing rather than the suits typical of Burawi senators.

=== Speaking style ===

Sago speaks in a rural Sago dialect. Former associates have noted that his accent was less pronounced earlier in his career. He is known for his volume — frequently projecting his voice at rallies without amplification — and for his opening greeting, '''"Walaalkay!"''' ({{lang|bur|"My brothers!"}}), which he uses at every public address.

=== "10 raises every 10 months" ===

Sago's most recognised slogan is '''"10 raises every 10 months"''' ({{lang|bur|''toban kordhin, toban bilood''}}), a promise to deliver ten per cent wage increases every ten months. The proposal has been described by economists, including those at the [[University of Sago]] where Sago formerly lectured, as not feasible. The slogan is chanted by supporters at SRP rallies. Sago has not published a fiscal plan for the proposal.

=== Economics and rhetoric ===

Sago's background as an economics lecturer occasionally surfaces in his public rhetoric. He has been observed in debates and interviews to begin an argument in an analytical register before shifting to the informal, combative style that characterises his public persona.

== Personal life ==

=== Name ===

Sago was born '''Bile Masar'''. The Masar family is distantly related to the [[House of Masar]], the merchant dynasty of [[Mazar]] that played a role in the founding of the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] through the [[Mazar-Iskhal Alliance]] of 403 AD. Sago changed his surname upon joining the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] in the late 1990s, which he has described as a statement of identification with the [[Sago]] region.

=== First marriage ===

Sago's first wife, Halgan Masar (née Koofur), died in 1985 in the [[Kedam train derailment]], in which a long-distance passenger train travelling between [[Sago]] and [[Dimarga]] derailed in the mountains. The circumstances of her death have been the subject of public speculation, though no investigation has established any connection to Sago. The derailment occurred during the period in which Sago withdrew from political life.

=== Second marriage and infidelity scandal ===

Sago married [[Ubax Sago]] (née Koofur), a niece of his first wife Halgan, on 8 December 2009, following their joint victory in the '''"Love Tour"''' campaign (see [[#Commune and Senate elections|Commune and Senate elections]]). Ubax Koofur was born in the same year as her aunt Halgan's death and had known Bile Sago for the entirety of her life through the family connection. The relationship attracted controversy due to the familial link, the 22-year age difference, and the fact that Sago had known Ubax since her infancy. The couple's eldest son, [[Guled Sago]], had been born in 2006, when Ubax was twenty-one and Bile was forty-three.

Ubax Sago serves, as of 2025, as the [[community (Burawa)|commune representative]] for '''Dafar Sare-1''' — the same seat her husband held before his election to the Senate. She was a member of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] until the founding of the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]] in 2020, at which point she joined her husband's party.

The couple have two children. Their eldest son, [[Guled Sago]] (born 2006), named after Bile's father [[Guled Masar]], is a political commentator.

In the mid-2010s, reports emerged that Sago had fathered a son in [[Balisca]] whom he had not publicly acknowledged. The reports were accompanied by accounts of frequent travel to Balisca and attendance at adult entertainment establishments. The infidelity scandal, combined with the cabaret controversy, contributed to the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]]'s decision to expel him in 2016. Sago has neither confirmed nor denied the reports; he has not travelled to Balisca since the scandal.

